I've observed an anomaly in call chain recording that appears to be
attributable to gcc's [mis]handling of frame pointers, and would appreciate
any feedback from perf maintainers or long-time users on whether this is a
new problem, a known issue, or some mistake on my part.

Context:
   x86_64
   Linux mainline kernel version 3.6.7
   gcc version 4.7.2

When the attached test program is compiled with
  gcc -std=gnu99 -O0 -g -fno-omit-frame-pointer -o val4_O0 val4.c
and profiled with
  perf record -e cpu-cycles -c 1000000 -g ./val4_O0
the report is as expected:

    50.11%  val4_O0  val4_O0            [.] fn1
            |
            --- fn1
                fn2
                main
                __libc_start_main

    49.17%  val4_O0  val4_O0            [.] fn2
            |
            --- fn2
                main
                __libc_start_main

However, when the program is instead compiled with
  gcc -std=gnu99 -O1 -g -fno-omit-frame-pointer -o val4_O1 val4.c
main disappears from half of the call chains:

    49.72%  val4_O1  val4_O1            [.] fn1
            |
            --- fn1
                fn2
                main
                __libc_start_main

    49.68%  val4_O1  val4_O1            [.] fn2
            |
            --- fn2
                __libc_start_main

Downstream impact includes incorrect accounting of total time by gprof2dot,
as shown in the attached dot graphs.

Reviewing the disassemblies of fn2 (attached), I gather that the critical
change was the movement of push %rbp from the top of the function down to
just before the call of fn1.

OProfile documents the known issue that samples taken before the call frame
is constructed will have an anomalous call chain
(http://oprofile.sourceforge.net/doc/interpreting-callgraph.html), but what
happened here seems worse than par for that course.  The "untimely" sampling
occurs as the rule rather than the exception.  In the absence of any further
information, I'd be inclined to say that gcc broke the contract for what
no-omit-frame-pointer should do.  Yes, the frame pointer is there, but it's
managed in a way that defeats the purpose (or at least, perf's purpose).

Thanks for any feedback on whether this a gcc bug, old news, or a mistake on
my part,
-- 
David Flater, National Institute of Standards and Technology, U.S.A.

